@section('content')
  <MarketingPageHero
    tone="protection"
    icon="i-f7-lock-shield-fill"
    title="Your Mac stays your business"
    lede="SystemCleaner reads your system to show you what is there, and sends none of it anywhere."
  />

  <article class="site-container">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What runs on your Mac</h2>
      <p>Every scan happens on the machine you are sitting at. Disk analysis, process sampling, startup item discovery, extension inventory, and cleanup all read the local filesystem and local system APIs. There is no remote scanning service and no upload step.</p>

      <h2>What the app reads</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Storage.</strong> File and folder sizes under your home directory and the paths you open, so the disk map can be drawn.</li>
        <li><strong>Processes.</strong> Process names, owners, CPU, and memory, refreshed while a process view is open.</li>
        <li><strong>Startup items.</strong> Launch agents and daemons in the standard user, system, and daemon locations.</li>
        <li><strong>Browser extensions.</strong> Extension manifests inside the browser profiles already on the machine.</li>
        <li><strong>Update availability.</strong> On its own, the app reads only what macOS and Homebrew already know about pending updates. Pantry's registry and the App Store are asked when you open Updates or Installed Apps.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What never leaves</h2>
      <p>None of the above is transmitted, logged remotely, or written to any service. There is no telemetry, no crash reporter, no analytics script, and no advertising identifier. The app does not require an account, so there is nothing to attach a profile to.</p>

      <h2>When the app talks to the network</h2>
      <p>SystemCleaner reaches the network in three situations, and only one of them happens on its own:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A daily check for a new version of SystemCleaner.</strong> About once a day the app fetches one small file from the latest release on GitHub to see whether a newer version is out. It sends nothing about your Mac; GitHub sees an ordinary download from your network address. It is on by default and you can turn it off in Settings, under General, with "Check for updates automatically". Nothing downloads or installs until you choose to.</li>
        <li><strong>Checking your other software for updates</strong>, when you open Updates or Installed Apps. That asks Apple, the App Store, Homebrew and Pantry what is newer than what you have installed.</li>
        <li><strong>Downloading a new version of SystemCleaner</strong>, when you press Download in Software Update.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Opening the app, scanning and cleaning send nothing anywhere, and none of these requests carries information about your Mac or its contents.</p>

      <h2>The marketing site</h2>
      <p>This website sets no tracking cookies and runs no analytics. If you subscribe to release notes, your email address is stored for that mailing list and used for nothing else. Unsubscribe links are in every message.</p>

      <h2>Verifying any of this</h2>
      <p>The app is open source. Every scan path, every network call, and every delete operation is readable in the repository, and the cleanup code lists its target directories explicitly rather than globbing your disk.</p>
      <p><a class="source-link" href="https://github.com/stacksjs/system-cleaner" rel="noreferrer">Read the source <span class="i-f7-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"></span></a></p>
    </div>
  </article>

